WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.040 --> 00:00:07.040
This tiny insect is called&nbsp;
a sharpshooter. Why you ask?

00:00:07.040 --> 00:00:15.520
Well, sharpshooters fling their pee and what's&nbsp;
more they do it using some pretty nifty physics&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:15.520 --> 00:00:21.640
called super-propulsion. And according to&nbsp;
the researchers studying these critters,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:21.640 --> 00:00:29.960
they may be the only known animal that can do it.

00:00:36.320 --> 00:00:42.280
Sharpshooters feed by sucking up nutrients&nbsp;
from xylem, the tubes which plants use to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:42.280 --> 00:00:50.440
transport water. But xylem fluid is about 95%&nbsp;
water, so to get enough calories sharpshooters&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:50.440 --> 00:00:59.920
need to consume a lot. And drinking that much&nbsp;
water means making a lot of urine, so - flick.

00:01:00.440 --> 00:01:06.720
But why flick drops? Cicadas, fellow xylem&nbsp;
feeders and cousins of sharpshooters,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:06.720 --> 00:01:13.360
spray their urine in a much more conventional&nbsp;
way - so why not sharpshooters. Well micro-ct&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:13.360 --> 00:01:19.480
scans and studies using high-speed cameras are&nbsp;
revealing exactly what's going on and it turns&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:19.480 --> 00:01:30.400
out the answer all comes down to size and energy.&nbsp;
Sharpshooters are tiny and at this scale water is,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:30.400 --> 00:01:36.560
for want of a better word, sticky. Sharpshooters&nbsp;
would risk being engulfed in their own pee if they&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:36.560 --> 00:01:43.480
didn't find a way to jettison it, but when you're&nbsp;
that small creating a jet of water uses a lot&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:43.480 --> 00:01:50.960
of energy. Far more than the teeny sharpshooters&nbsp;
could muste,r so they took a different approach.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:50.960 --> 00:01:56.840
The urine is gently squeezed into a droplet which&nbsp;
perches on hydrophobic hairs at the tip of an&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:56.840 --> 00:02:03.000
appendage called a stylet. the sharpshooters&nbsp;
then pull back the stylet like a spring and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:03.000 --> 00:02:10.360
release it flinging the drops with incredible&nbsp;
speed. But the droplets don't just move fast,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:10.360 --> 00:02:17.440
they move 40% faster than the stylet does.&nbsp;
This phenomenon is called super-propulsion,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:17.440 --> 00:02:24.600
but how does it work? Well imagine a diver&nbsp;
bouncing on a diving board. As the board bends it&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:24.600 --> 00:02:31.760
stores energy then releases it as it bounces back&nbsp;
up. If the dive then times a jump at the right&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:31.760 --> 00:02:38.200
moment they can make use of the energy stores in&nbsp;
the board to amplify that jump, traveling higher&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:38.200 --> 00:02:45.040
and faster. And it turns out something similar&nbsp;
is happening with the sharpshooter. Much like the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:45.040 --> 00:02:52.920
diving board tiny water droplets have the capacity&nbsp;
to deform and store energy in surface tension. As&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:52.920 --> 00:02:59.800
the stylet then moves it compresses the droplet&nbsp;
which then bounces back - time it exactly right&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:59.800 --> 00:03:05.560
right and this bounce back amplifies the force of&nbsp;
the stylet and the droplet flies away at a greater&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:05.560 --> 00:03:12.600
speed. Modeling has shown that achieving this&nbsp;
effect requires extremely accurate timing - too&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:12.600 --> 00:03:17.960
slow or too fast and you risk the bounce back&nbsp;
actually counteracting the force of the stylet,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:17.960 --> 00:03:24.200
slowing down the flick. And yet sharpshooters&nbsp;
appear remarkably capable of hitting the right&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:24.200 --> 00:03:32.160
rhythm every single time - saving them energy and&nbsp;
allowing them to keep feeding. Super-propulsion Is&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:32.160 --> 00:03:37.560
not a new concept in the lab but the team behind&nbsp;
this work say that this is the first time it's&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:37.560 --> 00:03:43.120
been seen in a natural system and that similar&nbsp;
approaches could be used to remove unwanted&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:43.120 --> 00:03:59.680
water from devices, like smartphones or smart&nbsp;
watches. All inspired by the humble sharpshooter.

